      explanation: `<div style="--mx-navy:#01506e; --mx-blue:#207bb5; --mx-lightblue:#0099d4; --mx-yellow:#e9dc12; --mx-grey:#4B556A; --mx-rule:#dde4ea; --mx-tint:#f2f7fa; --mx-body:#232a33; color:var(--mx-body); line-height:1.6"><div style="background:var(--mx-tint); border-left:4px solid var(--mx-yellow); padding:13px 18px; margin-bottom:18px"><strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">A. $8,120 is correct.</strong> Only the amounts available for immediate use qualify: the bank balance, petty cash and the unrestricted portion of the money market account.</div><h3 style="color:var(--mx-navy); font-size:17px; margin:0 0 8px">The rule</h3><p style="margin:0 0 12px">Cash and cash equivalents covers what a company can spend right now: coin and currency on hand, <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">petty cash</strong> funds, demand deposits, and short-term, highly liquid investments with an <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">original maturity of three months or less</strong>. A money market account meets that definition, so its unrestricted balance is a cash equivalent. The test is <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">availability</strong>: if the company cannot use the money today, it does not belong in the caption. The bank balance and petty cash need no adjustment, they are already spendable.</p><p style="margin:0 0 18px">Two items here fail that test. <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">Postdated checks</strong> are not cash because the funds cannot be drawn until the date on the check arrives; they stay in <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">accounts receivable</strong>. A <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">compensating balance</strong> tied to a loan due in three years is <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">restricted cash</strong>, and because the related debt is long-term, that $5,000 is reported as a <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">noncurrent asset</strong>. It is still an asset, it is just reported outside the cash caption and disclosed. The other $5,000 of the account carries no restriction and is still counted.</p><h3 style="color:var(--mx-navy); font-size:17px; margin:0 0 8px">The math</h3><div style="background:var(--mx-tint); border-left:4px solid var(--mx-blue); padding:8px 10px; margin-bottom:18px"><table style="border:0; border-collapse:collapse; width:100%"><tbody><tr><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px">Cash in bank</td><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px; text-align:right">$3,000</td></tr><tr><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px">Petty cash</td><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px; text-align:right">120</td></tr><tr><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px">Money market account</td><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px; text-align:right">10,000</td></tr><tr><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px">Less: restricted compensating balance</td><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px; text-align:right">(5,000)</td></tr><tr><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px">Postdated checks held by the cashier</td><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px; text-align:right">0</td></tr><tr><td style="border:0; padding:11px 14px"><strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">Cash and cash equivalents reported</strong></td><td style="border:0; padding:11px 14px; text-align:right"><strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">$8,120</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="margin:0 0 12px">The $500 of checks moves into cash only when the check date arrives. Watch the loan term on a compensating balance: if the related borrowing were current, the restricted amount would still be excluded from cash but shown as a current asset.</p></div>`,
